# Integrality of ̄ j(q^N) over K(̄ j(q))

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.isIntegral_jqNModC_of_modularPolynomialData`
(`304132bd-0186-56a2-ab84-233c89872e13`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `57c215e0-6b47-59e6-8771-8b6a898b15fe`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field, let $N$ be a natural number that is nonzero, and let `data` be a
modular-polynomial datum of level $N$: a polynomial $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ which is monic as a
polynomial in $Y$, whose degree in $Y$ equals $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}}
N/d$, and which satisfies $\Phi(j(q), j(q^N)) = 0$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, the substitution of $j(q)$
for $X$ in the coefficients being the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}[X] \to \mathbb{Q}((q))$ given by
`evalAtJ`. Here $\bar j(q) =$ `jqModC K` is the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K[[q]]$
of the integral power series $E_4^3 \eta^{-24}$ under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Z} \to K$,
and $\bar j(q^N) =$ `jqNModC K N` is its image under `qExpand K N`, the ring endomorphism of
$K((q))$ multiplying all exponents by $N$, that is, the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$. The conclusion
is that $\bar j(q^N)$ is integral over the intermediate field of $K((q))$ over $K$ generated by
$\bar j(q)$, namely $K(\bar j(q))$.

This is the integrality half of the classical statement that $\bar j(q^N)$ generates a finite
extension of $K(\bar j(q))$ inside $K((q))$, obtained by transferring the modular equation
$\Phi_N(j(q), j(q^N)) = 0$ from characteristic zero to an arbitrary field $K$ along the coefficient
map $\mathbb{Z} \to K$ and using monicity in the second variable. It feeds the irreducibility and
degree computations for $K(\bar j(q))(\bar j(q^N))$, in particular the statement that this extension
has degree $\psi(N)$ in characteristic zero, and the construction of places attached to a vanishing
modular polynomial.

